use warnings;
use strict;

=head1 NAME

freebsd_entity - snmpd pass_persist for ENTITY-MIB, ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB, and ENTITY-STATE-MIB on FreeBSD

=head1 VERSION

0.1.0

=cut

our $VERSION = '0.1.0';

=head1 SYNOPSIS

freebsd_entity B<-u> [B<-d> <cache dir>] [B<-i> <regexp>]... [B<-I> <regexp>]...
[B<--sensors>] [B<--power>] [B<--inventory>] [B<--state>] [B<--ipmi>] [B<--thresholds>]

freebsd_entity B<-p> <base OID> [B<-d> <cache dir>]

freebsd_entity B<-w> [B<-d> <cache dir>]

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Reports pluggable transceivers, SFP, QSFP, and the like, as found via
C<ifconfig -v>, as ENTITY-MIB inventory and their digital optical
monitoring readings as ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB sensors. Optionally also reports
temperature sensors, batteries and AC power, hardware inventory, BMC
sensors via IPMI, ENTITY-STATE-MIB state, and sensor thresholds.

It works in two parts. Ran with B<-u> from cron as root, it gathers
everything and writes the cache. Ran with B<-p> by snmpd, it answers
requests from the cache. snmpd never waits on reading the transceivers,
which can be slow on some drivers.

This is for net-snmp from ports, net-mgmt/net-snmp. bsnmpd from base does
not support pass_persist.

=head1 SNMPD CONFIG

    # /etc/crontab
    */5 * * * * root /usr/local/etc/snmp/extends/freebsd_entity -u --sensors --power --inventory --state

    # /usr/local/etc/snmp/snmpd.conf
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.47 /usr/local/etc/snmp/extends/freebsd_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.47
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.99 /usr/local/etc/snmp/extends/freebsd_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.99
    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.2.1.131 /usr/local/etc/snmp/extends/freebsd_entity -p .1.3.6.1.2.1.131

One line is needed per MIB as they are in separate trees. B<-p> is told
the tree as snmpd does not pass along what it was registered for. The
.1.3.6.1.2.1.131 line is only needed with B<--state>. B<--thresholds>
needs one more, see L</THRESHOLDS>.

Run B<-u> once before restarting snmpd so the cache exists. Without a
cache, nothing is returned.

=head1 REQUIREMENTS

A NIC and driver that support reading the module EEPROM for transceivers,
such as ix, ixl, mce, and cxgbe. Readings only show up for modules that
support digital optical monitoring.

dmidecode for the CPUs and memory with B<--inventory>.

freeipmi for B<--ipmi>, plus ipmi(4) loaded, which can be done at boot by
adding ipmi_load="YES" to /boot/loader.conf.

=head1 FLAGS

=head2 -u

Update the cache. Transceivers are always checked. The rest are added
with the flags below.

=head2 --sensors

Add the temperatures from coretemp(4), amdtemp(4), pchtherm(4), and the
ACPI thermal zones.

=head2 --power

Add the ACPI batteries, with their voltage and rate, and the AC adapter.

=head2 --inventory

Add the motherboard, CPUs, memory, and disks to the inventory. The
motherboard comes from kenv. The CPUs and memory need dmidecode and are
skipped without it. Disks come from geom, skipping md and cd devices.

=head2 --state

Add ENTITY-STATE-MIB state for the transceivers, the BMC with B<--ipmi>,
and the batteries and AC adapter with B<--power>.

=head2 --ipmi

Add the BMC sensors via C<ipmi-sensors> from freeipmi. Temperature,
voltage, current, power, and fan readings are reported, under a BMC
entity. Discrete sensors and those in other units, such as percent, are
skipped.

With B<--state>, the BMC gets a entStateTable row with its alarm set from
the IPMI thresholds. See L</STATE>.

=head2 --thresholds

Add the CISCO-ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB entSensorThresholdTable, under
.1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.91, for sensors that have thresholds. Currently only the
B<--ipmi> sensors do. See L</THRESHOLDS>.

=head2 -p <base OID>

Run as a snmpd pass_persist for the tree under the base OID.

=head2 -w

Print the cache the same as snmpwalk would, for debugging.

=head2 -d <cache dir>

The cache directory. Defaults to /var/cache/freebsd_entity. The cache is
entity.json in it, with state.json holding what is needed between runs.

=head2 -i <regexp>

Only check interfaces with a name matching this regexp for transceivers.
May be used more than once, in which case an interface matching any of
them is checked.

=head2 -I <regexp>

Skip interfaces with a name matching this regexp. May be used more than
once. Checked after B<-i>. Handy for skipping the piles of epair and tap
interfaces jails and VMs bring.

=head1 ENTITIES

Indexes are kept the same between runs, so a sensor or part keeps its
history. The ifindex is read via ifmib(4), so it matches IF-MIB.

- 1 :: The chassis, using what is in kenv.

- $ifindex * 1000 :: A transceiver port. entAliasMappingIdentifier maps it
  to IF-MIB::ifIndex.$ifindex. entPhysicalAlias is the interface
  description, as set via C<ifconfig $interface description ...>.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 1 :: The transceiver. Vendor name, part number,
  serial, and date go in entPhysicalMfgName, ModelName, SerialNum, and
  MfgDate.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 10 :: Transceiver temperature, celsius.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 11 :: Transceiver voltage, voltsDC.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 100 + $lane :: Laser bias current, amperes at milli scale.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 200 + $lane :: Tx power, other with dBm as the units.

- $ifindex * 1000 + 300 + $lane :: Rx power, other with dBm as the units.

- 1000000000 and up :: Everything else. Disk names and the like can change
  between boots, so each is given the next free index the first time it is
  seen, keyed by something stable such as the CAM bus, target, and LUN.
  That is saved in state.json. Removing it renumbers everything.

$lane is 0 for single lane transceivers and the lane number for multi lane
ones, such as 1 to 4 for QSFP. Interfaces with a ifindex over 999999 are
skipped so the transceiver indexes stay under 1000000000.

The transceiver Tx and Rx power sensors are named "$interface Tx Power
Sensor" and "$interface Rx Power Sensor", or "$interface Lane $lane Rx
Power Sensor" and so on for multi lane ones. ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB lacks a
dBm type and LibreNMS uses those names to treat them as dBm. A power
reading of zero, -inf dBm, is reported as -40 dBm.

Battery charge is not reported, as ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB lacks a percent type.

=head1 THRESHOLDS

ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB has nowhere to put thresholds, so with B<--thresholds>
they go in the entSensorThresholdTable from CISCO-ENTITY-SENSOR-MIB. It is
indexed by the entPhysicalIndex of the sensor, then the threshold.

- 1 :: Lower non-recoverable, critical, less or equal.

- 2 :: Lower critical, major, less or equal.

- 3 :: Lower non-critical, minor, less or equal.

- 4 :: Upper non-critical, minor, greater or equal.

- 5 :: Upper critical, major, greater or equal.

- 6 :: Upper non-recoverable, critical, greater or equal.

Only the ones the BMC has are present. The value uses the same scale and
precision as the sensor's entPhySensorValue. entSensorThresholdEvaluation
is true if the reading is past it. entSensorThresholdNotificationEnable is
always false, as no traps are sent.

It needs its own pass_persist line...

    pass_persist .1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.91 /usr/local/etc/snmp/extends/freebsd_entity -p .1.3.6.1.4.1.9.9.91

=head1 STATE

With B<--state>, the entStateTable has a row for each transceiver, with
B<--power> each battery and the AC adapter, and with B<--ipmi> the BMC.

- Transceivers :: entStateAdmin is unlocked if the interface is up and
  locked if down. entStateOper is enabled. entStateAlarm is always clear,
  as ifconfig does not report the alarm, loss of signal, or Tx fault flags.

- Batteries :: entStateAlarm has critical set if acpiconf reports the
  state as critical.

- AC adapter :: entStateOper is enabled if on AC power and disabled if not.

- BMC :: entStateOper is enabled. entStateAlarm has critical set if any
  sensor is at or past a non-recoverable threshold, major for a critical
  one, and warning for a non-critical one.

The rest of the columns are unknown.

=head1 INSTALL

    pkg install p5-JSON p5-File-Slurp dmidecode

    # for --ipmi
    pkg install freeipmi

=cut

